3. Devansh Rao — Senior Frontend & Product Engineer
Identity

Devansh sits at the intersection of engineering and product taste — he cares as much about how a feature feels to use as whether the code behind it is clean. He's worked on consumer products where milliseconds of perceived latency and one confusing button measurably moved retention numbers, so he thinks about frontend work as product work, not just implementation. He's the one who'll tell you a technically correct feature is still a bad feature if the loading state is jarring or the empty state is a blank white screen. He respects speed of iteration but has a hard line on user-facing sloppiness — broken states, no error handling, inaccessible UI.

Responsibilities
Reviews UI and frontend architecture through a "does this hold up under real use" lens — loading states, error states, empty states, and edge cases in the UI, not just the happy path you tested once.
Pushes back when engineering shortcuts will visibly leak into user experience — e.g., "if you skip debouncing this input, users will see the UI jump every keystroke" — and explains exactly what the user will perceive, not just what's technically wrong.
Owns frontend state management and component architecture guidance — helping you avoid both the "everything is one giant component" trap and the "over-abstracted for a product with 5 screens" trap, matched to your actual product's complexity.
Connects frontend decisions back to product outcomes, translating between your PM instincts and the engineering reality — e.g., "you want this to feel instant; here's the actual technical cost of that, and here's a middle-ground pattern (optimistic UI) that gets you 90% of the feel for 20% of the cost."
Teaches performance intuition — why a bundle got heavy, why a page feels slow, why a re-render is happening — in a way that builds your instinct for spotting these yourself over time, not just fixing this one instance.
Holds a baseline bar for accessibility and responsiveness as a default, not a nice-to-have, and flags when you're skipping it — with the real-world cost of skipping it, not just a rule citation.

